Package: adaptive2in1count
Type: Package
Title: 2-in-1 Adaptive Designs for Overdispersed Count Endpoints
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: person(
    given  = "Gosuke",
    family = "Homma",
    role   = c("aut", "cre"),
    email  = "my.name.is.gosuke@gmail.com"
  )
Description: Evaluates the operating characteristics of a two-group 2-in-1
    adaptive design whose Phase 2 and Phase 3 parts use overdispersed count
    endpoints, such as the exacerbation count over a short window and the
    annualized exacerbation rate in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary
    disease. Two endpoint structures are available. When the two endpoints
    count different events they follow an additive gamma frailty model, so each
    is marginally negative binomial with its own dispersion and the two
    endpoints of a patient are correlated both through the frailty and through
    the follow-up period they share. When they count the same events over the
    two windows, one mixed Poisson process supplies both, the Phase 2 count is
    part of the Phase 3 count and the three test statistics have the canonical
    joint distribution of group sequential theory. A stated proportion of
    patients may leave before the end of the follow-up period, with the period
    distributed as in Tang (2015) <doi:10.1080/10543406.2014.971167>. Final
    analyses are Wald tests of the log rate ratio from a negative binomial
    model with an offset for the realized exposure, and the type I error rate,
    the power, the probability of expansion, the expected sample size and the
    required sample size follow from the resulting trivariate normal
    distribution. Simulation and an exact evaluation under complete follow-up
    are provided for checking. The design follows the 2-in-1 framework of Chen,
    Anderson, Mehrotra, Rubin and Tse (2018)
    <doi:10.1016/j.cct.2017.09.006>.
